WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:10.440
This is what it looks like to drive&nbsp;
through Wyoming… for about 400 miles.

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:13.960
About the only thing you’ll see during&nbsp;
the first four and a half hours are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:22.120
the towns of Rock Springs and Rawlins,&nbsp;
populations: 23,000 and 8,000, respectively.

00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:26.640
Hopefully you aren’t prone to&nbsp;
boredom because, quite frankly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:31.680
there’s nothing to see. Despite&nbsp;
being larger than the United Kingdom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:40.440
Wyoming is home to less than 600,000 people&nbsp;
– about 17% of 1% of the U.S. population.

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:44.600
And this emptiness is no accident.

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:49.280
Sponsored by Nebula, the exclusive&nbsp;
home to my Original series “China,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:54.840
Actually”. Stay tuned for&nbsp;
a new episode coming soon.

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:58.360
Coal is the lifeblood of Wyoming.

00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:03.560
The Cowboy State alone produces 40%&nbsp;
of America’s supply – three times&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:09.880
more than 2nd place West Virginia and&nbsp;
over 50% more than all of Appalachia.

00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:15.640
The North Antelope Rochelle Mine in the state’s&nbsp;
Northeast corner is the largest open-pit coal mine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:24.720
in the world. It operates on two 12-hour&nbsp;
shifts, rain or shine, 365 days a year.

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:29.840
Without it, the economy, and, indeed, the&nbsp;
Wyoming way of life, would soon collapse.

00:01:31.720 --> 00:01:36.040
Though not, as you might assume, for lack of jobs.

00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:41.960
Truth be told, there are only about&nbsp;
5,000 coal miners left in the state.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:47.680
The loss of these high-paying jobs certainly&nbsp;
wouldn’t help, but even the incomprehensibly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:57.120
massive North Antelope Mine only employs about&nbsp;
1,100 people – 0.1% of the state’s population.

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:02.360
No, the real reason Wyoming&nbsp;
needs coal are severance taxes.

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:07.920
For every hundred dollars worth of coal, oil,&nbsp;
and gas produced in the state, between six&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:15.520
and six and a half dollars are siphoned into the&nbsp;
public purse, creating an enormous budget surplus.

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:19.880
Rather than redistribute this&nbsp;
wealth directly as annual dividends,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.880 --> 00:02:25.680
as Alaska does, the people of&nbsp;
Wyoming have chosen low taxes.

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:31.840
In any given year, its average household&nbsp;
pays only about $3,500 in property, sales,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:39.920
and use taxes, but receives about $27,000&nbsp;
worth of services – including education,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:42.720
healthcare, and infrastructure.

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:47.720
In other words, Wyoming residents&nbsp;
receive an 80-90% discount on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.720 --> 00:02:52.920
public services – all thanks to fossil fuels.

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:58.920
But this unusual arrangement&nbsp;
has some unusual implications.

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:05.320
Imagine you move to, say, North Dakota.&nbsp;
This line represents your contributions to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:12.240
the state in the form of taxes, and this&nbsp;
one, the services you receive in return.

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:16.920
What this graph, by the economist&nbsp;
Dr. Peter Evangelakis, shows is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:22.360
new residents have a slightly positive&nbsp;
effect on the state budget. On average,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:28.600
and in the vast majority of states,&nbsp;
newcomers give more than they take.

00:03:28.600 --> 00:03:35.360
Now let’s say you move to nearby Wyoming.&nbsp;
There, you’d still consume one person’s-worth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:43.200
of resources. Yet, because its tax rates are so&nbsp;
low, you wouldn’t add much additional revenue.

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:47.560
New residents are a massive drain on its budget.

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:54.680
Coal is finite. The larger the population,&nbsp;
the less pie is left for everyone.

00:03:54.680 --> 00:04:01.920
Simply put: its taxpayers have a uniquely&nbsp;
strong incentive to keep their state empty.

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:07.960
While nearly every region of America has slowly&nbsp;
urbanized – including Red states like Arkansas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.960 --> 00:04:15.280
Texas, and Idaho – Wyoming stands apart as one&nbsp;
of the last true remnants of the Wild West.

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:22.960
In 1980, it was the 49th most populous state&nbsp;
in the Union. Today, it’s the 50th. In those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:31.320
40 years, its net population grew by&nbsp;
just 2,552 people a year on average.

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:36.040
There is no Austin, Texas island&nbsp;
of liberalism, or Bentonville,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.040 --> 00:04:44.200
Arkansas pocket of urban growth. Every&nbsp;
inch of Wyoming remains just… Wyoming.

00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:49.280
And this resistance to change is&nbsp;
not limited to demographic growth.

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:52.040
Take, clean energy, for example.

00:04:52.040 --> 00:04:58.280
In the last 15 years, wind farms have rapidly&nbsp;
proliferated across the United States – and not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.280 --> 00:05:05.280
just including but especially in traditionally&nbsp;
Red states like Texas, Oklahoma, and Kansas.

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:07.640
But not Wyoming.

00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:14.080
Between 2011 and 19, the amount of wind power&nbsp;
generated by the Cowboy State actually decreased&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:21.480
by nearly 10% as few new turbines were&nbsp;
built while many were taken out of service.

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:28.880
In a 2023 survey of 8 Western states – 5 Red&nbsp;
and 3 Blue – Wyoming was the only one where a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:36.400
majority of respondents preferred to “maximize”,&nbsp;
not “reduce the need” for coal, oil, and gas.

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:42.440
Wyoming makes Montana look like a&nbsp;
bastion of Prius-driving liberals.

00:05:42.440 --> 00:05:47.040
But all this may be about to change.

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:50.800
On August 30th, 2022, it became clear California&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.800 --> 00:05:55.480
was about to experience one of&nbsp;
its hottest weeks in history.

00:05:55.480 --> 00:05:58.360
100 plus degree temperatures were forecast every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.360 --> 00:06:04.400
day for the Greater Los Angeles region&nbsp;
– home to nearly 20 million Americans.

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:07.240
Extreme heat would surely lead to extreme use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.240 --> 00:06:12.040
of air conditioning – one of the&nbsp;
biggest consumers of electricity.

00:06:12.040 --> 00:06:18.160
This line represents energy use on a typical&nbsp;
Summer day. This one, the record for all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:27.080
of 2021. And these, actual usage between&nbsp;
August 31st and September 6th last year.

00:06:27.080 --> 00:06:32.320
At the same time, electricity&nbsp;
supply was seriously constrained.

00:06:32.320 --> 00:06:37.400
Blistering temperatures led to&nbsp;
droughts. Droughts led to wildfires,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.400 --> 00:06:44.320
and wildfires to smoke, which reduced the&nbsp;
efficiency of solar panels by roughly 30%.

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:49.320
Droughts also brought water levels to&nbsp;
record lows in reservoirs across the state,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.320 --> 00:06:55.680
including the Hoover Dam’s Lake Mead, a major&nbsp;
source of power for Southern California.

00:06:55.680 --> 00:06:59.960
If this unusually large demand looked&nbsp;
as though it might exceed this unusually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.960 --> 00:07:07.360
low supply – even momentarily – state officials&nbsp;
would have no choice but to initiate blackouts.

00:07:07.360 --> 00:07:12.640
And, on Tuesday, this nightmare&nbsp;
scenario seemed inevitable.

00:07:12.640 --> 00:07:17.440
By late afternoon, Sacramento, San Jose,&nbsp;
Santa Rosa, Redwood City, Livermore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.440 --> 00:07:23.400
Napa, and King City had all tied or&nbsp;
broken their all-time heat records.

00:07:23.400 --> 00:07:27.040
Sports games were canceled,&nbsp;
recess was held inside,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.040 --> 00:07:32.840
and BART trains operated at reduced&nbsp;
speeds in case tracks were warped by heat.

00:07:32.840 --> 00:07:37.480
A few minutes before 5, as Californians&nbsp;
began returning home from work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.480 --> 00:07:43.880
demand for electricity set a new state&nbsp;
record, flirting with the edge of supply.

00:07:43.880 --> 00:07:48.200
In a panic, Governor Gavin Newsom&nbsp;
sent an emergency text message,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.200 --> 00:07:51.800
begging residents to reduce their usage.

00:07:51.800 --> 00:07:56.200
Either thousands of households would&nbsp;
voluntarily turn down their ACs on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.200 --> 00:08:03.240
hottest day of the year, or the state was about to&nbsp;
experience its third major blackout in 20 years.

00:08:03.240 --> 00:08:06.680
It worked. Barely. Within minutes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.680 --> 00:08:12.840
about 2,000 megawatts of demand&nbsp;
disappeared, narrowly averting crisis.

00:08:12.840 --> 00:08:16.000
But harder times lie ahead.

00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:21.120
Like many states, California is&nbsp;
planning for a clean energy future.

00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:26.080
By 2026, half of its electricity&nbsp;
must come from renewable sources,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.080 --> 00:08:32.040
followed by 60% in 2030, and 100% by 2045.

00:08:32.040 --> 00:08:37.520
That means a greater reliance on solar and&nbsp;
hydro power – both of which are weakened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.520 --> 00:08:43.280
at the precise moment energy use is&nbsp;
at its highest – hot summer days.

00:08:43.280 --> 00:08:50.400
To achieve its climate goals without repeating the&nbsp;
events of last September, California needs help.

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:56.640
In short: there’s a huge market for the right&nbsp;
kind of energy at the right time of day.

00:08:56.640 --> 00:09:07.440
And Wyoming, it turns out, has&nbsp;
just what California needs.

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:12.040
Cruel, relentless wind is&nbsp;
a fact of life in Wyoming.

00:09:12.040 --> 00:09:16.720
Its narrow mountain ranges and high&nbsp;
plateaus create thousands of natural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.720 --> 00:09:22.360
funnels for jet streams, which blow&nbsp;
wind Eastward across the United States.

00:09:22.360 --> 00:09:28.840
When these funnels are perpendicular to highways,&nbsp;
as is the case here on I-25 south of Wheatland,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.840 --> 00:09:35.400
winds can so violently strike the side of&nbsp;
a car – or semi-truck – as to flip it over.

00:09:35.400 --> 00:09:37.720
This happens surprisingly frequently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.720 --> 00:09:43.880
as seen in this video, which resulted&nbsp;
– miraculously – in zero injuries.

00:09:43.880 --> 00:09:49.680
With its high-altitude prairie and sparse human&nbsp;
habitation, there’s simply nothing to slow wind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.680 --> 00:09:57.600
down, allowing it to reach 65 miles an hour or&nbsp;
faster, at which point roads are typically closed.

00:09:57.600 --> 00:10:03.880
While this effect creates some white-knuckle&nbsp;
driving, it’s perfect for wind farms.

00:10:03.880 --> 00:10:10.280
Wind speeds are classified between&nbsp;
1 (poor) and 7 (superb). Half of all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.280 --> 00:10:15.960
6 and 7-class wind in the nation is&nbsp;
located in Wyoming. And most of that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.960 --> 00:10:20.320
in just a few counties around&nbsp;
the state’s southeast corner.

00:10:20.320 --> 00:10:25.600
Not only that, but its wind is strong when&nbsp;
California’s is the weakest – right here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.600 --> 00:10:34.120
in the afternoon. And when the sun is down on the&nbsp;
West Coast, the Wyoming wind is working overtime.

00:10:34.120 --> 00:10:37.480
This unlikely alliance – between&nbsp;
the country’s largest and one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.480 --> 00:10:40.280
its most liberal states and its smallest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.280 --> 00:10:46.200
most conservative one – has the potential&nbsp;
to radically transform the American West.

00:10:46.200 --> 00:10:50.960
The only thing that stands in the way is&nbsp;
an arduous bureaucratic maze of federal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.960 --> 00:10:54.160
state, and county regulations.

00:10:54.160 --> 00:11:00.680
In 2010, the state of Wyoming re-classified wind&nbsp;
farms as “industrial facilities”, requiring that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.680 --> 00:11:07.120
they go through the same permitting process as&nbsp;
those involving, for example, radioactive waste.

00:11:07.120 --> 00:11:10.560
Only after submitting a 400+ page application,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.560 --> 00:11:15.840
paying up to $100,000 in fees, and holding&nbsp;
a public hearing, can a wind developer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.840 --> 00:11:22.360
proceed to step two: submitting another&nbsp;
application, this time to the county.

00:11:22.360 --> 00:11:27.120
At any point in this process, any person&nbsp;
or organization with a Wyoming address can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.120 --> 00:11:33.440
challenge the permit – giving NIMBYs&nbsp;
free rein to slow or halt progress.

00:11:33.440 --> 00:11:36.320
Then there’s the federal bureaucracy.

00:11:36.320 --> 00:11:39.040
No fewer than six such departments administer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.040 --> 00:11:44.040
Wyoming land – mainly the&nbsp;
Bureau of Land Management.

00:11:44.040 --> 00:11:47.760
In the 1880s, the U.S. government&nbsp;
incentivized the construction of new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.760 --> 00:11:53.720
railroads by granting those companies land on&nbsp;
either side of the tracks. In hopes that this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.720 --> 00:11:58.040
land would then be sold to individual&nbsp;
settlers rather than large companies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.040 --> 00:12:03.040
it was given to the railroads in&nbsp;
alternating square-mile sections.

00:12:03.040 --> 00:12:09.080
The result is a checkerboard of public and&nbsp;
private land in windy Southern Wyoming.

00:12:09.080 --> 00:12:14.040
Since four squares of BLM land surround&nbsp;
every one patch of private land,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.040 --> 00:12:18.160
dealing with the feds is simply unavoidable.

00:12:18.160 --> 00:12:20.840
Add all this up and any attempt to tap into this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.840 --> 00:12:25.600
treasure trove of wind power&nbsp;
should be dead in the water.

00:12:25.600 --> 00:12:30.280
Who would gamble hundreds of millions of&nbsp;
dollars in upfront costs alone in the hopes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.280 --> 00:12:34.640
that America’s most conservative state&nbsp;
would sign off on clean energy and that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.640 --> 00:12:40.680
not one of a dozen counties or hundreds&nbsp;
of landowners would veto the project?

00:12:40.680 --> 00:12:44.320
Enter billionaire oil tycoon,&nbsp;
Republican mega-donor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.320 --> 00:12:49.760
and future owner of America’s&nbsp;
largest wind farm, Philip Anschutz.

00:12:49.760 --> 00:12:54.400
His plan is to build 1,000 turbines,&nbsp;
capable of powering a million homes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.400 --> 00:13:00.760
on 320,000 acres in a place&nbsp;
named for coal: “Carbon County”

00:13:00.760 --> 00:13:04.480
A $3 billion 700-mile transmission line will then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.480 --> 00:13:10.920
connect this wind farm to California&nbsp;
through Colorado, Utah, and Nevada.

00:13:10.920 --> 00:13:16.240
It took 18 years to secure the permits&nbsp;
but approval was finally granted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.240 --> 00:13:19.600
against all odds, this spring.

00:13:19.600 --> 00:13:25.720
Today, after a ten-year stagnation,&nbsp;
Wyoming wind is making a comeback.

00:13:25.720 --> 00:13:28.320
About eight other farms are in development,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.320 --> 00:13:32.840
with most set to become&nbsp;
operational in the next 2 years.

00:13:32.840 --> 00:13:39.360
Only one question remains: Will&nbsp;
the people of Wyoming allow it?

00:13:39.360 --> 00:13:46.280
In the 1970s, the term “national sacrifice zone”&nbsp;
was coined to describe places like Wyoming.

00:13:46.280 --> 00:13:51.360
Out here, on the country’s remote frontier,&nbsp;
a few Americans would endure the sight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.360 --> 00:13:56.880
smell, and symptoms of coal so that&nbsp;
many more could enjoy its fruits.

00:13:56.880 --> 00:14:03.680
In return, these areas were well compensated&nbsp;
with high salaries and low taxes.

00:14:03.680 --> 00:14:10.640
But, for some, wind represents an unwelcome&nbsp;
renegotiation of that implicit trade.

00:14:10.640 --> 00:14:14.840
According to the University of Wyoming,&nbsp;
wind will bring somewhere between three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.840 --> 00:14:20.840
and nine thousand jobs to the state&nbsp;
– not at all an insignificant amount.

00:14:20.840 --> 00:14:27.040
The overwhelming majority, however, are short-term&nbsp;
construction jobs – building access roads,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.040 --> 00:14:30.600
transporting parts, and installing equipment.

00:14:30.600 --> 00:14:36.720
In the long term, there may only be 500 to&nbsp;
1,500 operations and maintenance roles – a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.720 --> 00:14:41.480
drop in the bucket compared to coal, oil, and gas.

00:14:41.480 --> 00:14:46.360
Now, Wyoming residents are powerless to&nbsp;
stop the spread of green energy – just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.360 --> 00:14:51.200
as global oil prices have long&nbsp;
dictated the state of its economy.

00:14:51.200 --> 00:14:55.960
But there is one thing they can do: tax it.

00:14:55.960 --> 00:15:03.560
Wyoming is already the only state in the union&nbsp;
with a wind tax. Currently $1 per megawatt-hour,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:03.560 --> 00:15:10.000
several politicians have proposed&nbsp;
raising it to 2, 3, 4, or even $5.

00:15:10.000 --> 00:15:15.680
It’s effectively playing a high-stakes&nbsp;
game of chicken with wind developers.

00:15:15.680 --> 00:15:21.560
Wyoming is betting that its wind is so good&nbsp;
that investors will swallow the extra cost.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:21.560 --> 00:15:27.680
And if it works, the tax could breathe new life&nbsp;
into Wyoming’s dying economy. By introducing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:27.680 --> 00:15:34.360
a new source of revenue, the state could&nbsp;
begin weaning itself off of fossil fuels.

00:15:34.360 --> 00:15:39.320
But if Wyoming overplays its hand and&nbsp;
developers take their money to Colorado&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:39.320 --> 00:15:48.360
or New Mexico instead, its economy could&nbsp;
be left more dependent on coal than ever.

00:15:48.360 --> 00:15:53.680
Perhaps the place in the world most&nbsp;
similar to Wyoming is Baotou, China.

00:15:53.680 --> 00:15:55.840
In fact, it’s hard to distinguish these clips&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:55.840 --> 00:16:00.200
of its coal mines from those of&nbsp;
Wyoming’s North Antelope mine.

00:16:00.200 --> 00:16:04.000
Baotou is also, in all likelihood,&nbsp;
where the rare earth metals found&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.000 --> 00:16:08.320
in the device you’re watching&nbsp;
this very video on, originated.

00:16:08.320 --> 00:16:13.280
For years, China was accused of artificially&nbsp;
subsidizing their production for political&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:13.280 --> 00:16:19.400
control. But the real reason for China’s&nbsp;
near monopoly is much more complicated.

00:16:19.400 --> 00:16:22.800
Find out why in my six-part&nbsp;
